> ## What the check on 9 Oct found
>
> Re-read every number before using it.
>
> - **Strongest results.**
>   - Raising labels clipped at an assay limit works on 6 datasets, including QM9. It is driven
>     by a known flag, not a detector.
>   - Its control deals the same lifts to the wrong clipped molecules. The per-molecule lift
>     beats that control in only 1 of 22 rows. So what works is raising the clipped labels by
>     about the right average amount, not the per-molecule assignment.
> - **Small molecules, real labels.** The only controlled gain above 0.012 RMSE is hERG Ki with
>   the network, moving flagged labels toward their neighbours' mean: 0.638 to 0.578.
> - **New, not in the outline.** Deleting the flagged reactions in Buchwald-Hartwig beats
>   matched random deletion: 20.08 to 18.04 RMSE, against 19.90 for random deletion.
> - **Antibody exception.**
>   - SMAC colloidal stability is the clearest, with controls. The support vector regressor
>     goes 0.2740 to 0.4034 Spearman, against 0.2782 for the shuffled control.
>   - Titer, CDR flexibility and anti-coronavirus affinity come next, partly controlled.
>   - Humanness and nanobody thermostability are not exceptions.
>   - The strong-row rule asks +0.05 Spearman on about 53 held-out antibodies. That is easier to
>     reach than 5% RMSE, so the exception looks larger than it is. Say so.
